  [1mThe fork:[0m an ID that identifies [3mthe person[0m can't be produced by the browser before login — login is what proves who
  they are. Anything the client invents beforehand identifies a [3mbrowser[0m, not a person. So either the ID comes back from
  the server and gets stored, or it's an ID the person already has and supplies.

  - [1mServer issues it, client stores it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m authenticates, the server returns the canonical user
    ID, you persist it and read it everywhere after. This is the standard shape and the only one where the ID is
    trustworthy. Cost: your login is a stub ([38;5;153mapp.js:6[39m), so there's no server to issue anything yet — we'd design against
    a defined response contract and fake it until the real endpoint exists. It also means [38;5;153muserId[39m is [3mnot[0m a parameter to
    [38;5;153mlogin()[39m; it's a parameter to everything that comes after login.
  - [1mPerson supplies it[0m — an external ID they already know (employee number, tenant ID, account number), typed at login
    and persisted. This is the only reading where [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m is genuinely correct. Cost: a
    user-supplied identifier is unverified until the server confirms it matches the credentials, so it can't be trusted
    for anything that matters until it is.
  - [1mBrowser-scoped ID, promoted at login[0m — mint a device/visitor ID on first load, persist it, and associate it with the
    real user once login succeeds. Buys pre-login tracking. Cost: two identifiers to reason about and keep straight;
    genuinely useful for analytics, overkill if you just want to know who's signed in.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m server-issued and stored. It's the only option where "who logged in" is an answer you can rely on,
  and it's what the other forms will actually want to read. The consequence I want you to say yes to explicitly: it
  means [38;5;153mlogin()[39m does [3mnot[0m get a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter — the thing you originally asked for — because the value doesn't exist
  until login returns.
